>Who are "they" and what laws have "they" made against pornography?  Last
>time I checked, non-obscene pornography was constitutionally protected.
>(And it was very rare for a court to deem something "obscene.")

Some people assume that only the US has anything to do with this issue.
This is not the case.  Certain countries (such as the UK, Germany, Japan,
Malaysia, China, and so on) have different criteria than the US regarding
what is considered "obscene."

In mainland China, for instance, swimsuit posters were illegal until last
year.

Japan's Obscenity Act of 1908 bans the import of any "pictures, sculptures,
and writing" which may be deemed "threatening toward the state" (although
in practice it is only applied to photographic media containing pubic hair,
and art photos are sometimes exempt).  From what I've heard, the UK uses a
similar law to limit hard-core pornography.

Malaysians who enter Malaysia with pornographic materials are liable to
be given the "green card" (i.e. criminal record).

Thus, even if a moderated "public domain" (hah!) pornography group were to
be established, sysadmins will still have to decide which groups to keep and
which to discard.

Please show some concern toward the regulations of non-US governments when
you post to USENET.

