I have an account on a machine in which all *.log files were deleted
whenever the disk got full. The idea is that *.log files are created
by TeX and you really don't need to keep them around.

Unfortunately for me, I didn't know about TeX log files, or the file
deletion policy, and decided to call name the files containing the
results of thesis experiments *.log. They were deleted. Problems with
the backups mean that they couldn't be recovered. I eventually
recreated them at a cost of several CPU days of computer time.

What I do now:
  Once a day, I run a program that finds all my files that might be deleted
  and sends me email.

  Once a day, I run a program that looks at "/etc/dumpdates" tells the
  time and level of the most recent backups. (The main machines are
  almost always backedup, but the networked workstations are sometimes
  missed.)

