Especially the last one was revealing, wasn't it? :-) But now, seriously:
In my legal counselling, I have frequently encountered unjustified fears
like those displayed by Mr. Deibele. Calmly and factually informing the
inquirer about the legal situation will not help, usually. The reason is
simple: we deal with what a psychologically trained acquaintance of mine
once correcly identified as a "projective fear". The client - or inquirer,
here - is feeling deeply uncomfortable with a certain issue or behaviour
or tendency and projects internal resistance to an outward institution,
to the criminal prosecution and justice system in our case.
   Note that I do *not* denounce any founded concern (like the touchy
copyright issue which could *indeed* make an admin decide to drop a
newsgroup like a.b.p.e), but rather a tendency - often not even realized
by the person involved - of building up a bogeyman to excuse taking an
action which is desired (if, e.g., the admin somhehow feels that assorted
smut was a bad thing and better be cut off), yet would be harder to
justify with the underlying real motives unveiled to himself or others.
This is especially true in a conflict, if e.g. an admin be deeply
opposed against censorship, yet strongly dislike a certain group.
 
