In <16B85BF21.C96@vm.urz.uni-heidelberg.de> C96@vm.urz.uni-heidelberg.de 
(Alexander EICHENER) writes:

>In my legal counselling, I have frequently encountered unjustified fears
>like those displayed by Mr. Deibele. Calmly and factually informing the
>inquirer about the legal situation will not help, usually. The reason is
>simple: we deal with what a psychologically trained acquaintance of mine
>once correcly identified as a "projective fear". The client - or inquirer,
>here - is feeling deeply uncomfortable with a certain issue or behaviour
>or tendency and projects internal resistance to an outward institution,
>to the criminal prosecution and justice system in our case.

My - this sounds cut and dried - as long as you make the assumption that the
news admin is just paranoid about a law (or laws) that do not apply. But
what if your client has been advised a law IS being violated?  Should the
same person be criticized for standing on the side of the law - even in the
face of most other people being unaware of it (or opposed to it)? Should
institutions be asked to perform functions that individuals have been
prosecuted for (or been threatened with prosecution if they continued)?  

I think the premise of Mr. Eichener's post is misleading.  If you are
responsible for Usenet News (or any other information service) either on a
personal bulletin board system or a major site you should be aware of the
federal/state/local laws that apply to your system, your clients, and you
personally.  You should be aware of the content of what your system is
serving out in areas that may violate those laws.  You should be aware of
the age of the audience (and potential audience) of your system.  If you are
low in an administrative structure you should keep your superiors aware of
what is happening at other sites in this area.  You should get legal advice
either from your institution's legal representative or from a private
(criminal) lawyer and decide if you want to follow it.

Too many people say "what I don't know won't hurt me" or "we don't have a
problem so I don't want to create one".  Fine - your decision.  But look
around.  I've been following this issue for a few years now.  It is a legal
(and often political) issue that keeps getting addressed in wider and
higher-level circles.  Are people doing this because it is "fun"?  No -
because it is a problem that has to be addressed at each site.

I guess by Mr. Eichener's analysis, I have a "projective fear" of driving my
car 55 MPH in a 25 MPH speed zone near a grade school.  I seem to know there
is a law - there is certainly a speed-limit sign - but I've never been
convicted for it (or even known anyone who has been caught for it).  Perhaps
someone would like me to think it is a "stupid law" not to be enforced on
weekends when there are no kids around.  Maybe others agree in principle
with the law but think it is too restrictive - the speed limit should be 40
MPH.  Maybe I hear a person from some other city who says "but the speed
limit IS 55 MPH in a school zone here!".  So, should I squelch this silly
"projective fear" and explain my reasoning to the judge?  No - I'll collect
all the facts and make my decision on who to listen to - as should everyone
else.  


