I totally agree with Mr. Deibele here.  I work at Iowa State University.  At
the University of Iowa (another state-run institution in Iowa) they have had
two major media events over the showing of two films on campus.  The first
occurred in late September of 1991, when a German class was shown a file in
which a clothed male caused a young boy to touch his groin area.  This was
played out in the major newspaper in Iowa (the Des Moines Register).  In the
end, the county attorney indicated anyone showing (or possessing) the file
was guilty of breaking the law.  The were warned they would be prosecuted
next time.

The second event occurred just a few weeks ago when a visiting artist showed
an art film containing oral homosexual sex to an art class.  Again, the
media covered it all - including the head of the Board of Regents (the
governing body for the state-run schools) who said somebody was going to get
fired if it happened again.  The school had to apologize to the class and
offer warnings prior to any other such film being shown again.  Once again,
the state obscenity law was brought up (I personally feel the "educational
escape clause would apply in this case, however).

My point here is the laws are NOT taken lightly by the governing bodies -
whether you agree with them or not.  I am certainly not going to take the
Iowa law (or Iowa public opinion) lightly.

>>[...] But it is also true that very many people are not too
>>concerned about it. [...]
>
>Most people are not going to be held responsible.  Admins and sysops will 
>certainly be the ones held accountable.  

Here again, I totally agree.  When a media (or legal) event happens to publicly
embarrass an institution, and somebody has to take the heat (be disciplined
or get fired) is it going to be the head of the institution?  No.  The
department head?  Maybe.  But who is under pressure? - the local Usenet News
admin.  Sure, I find a lot of people not concerned - mostly not concerned
about the local site's problem with the law or public opinion.  They just
want what they want - and want somebody else to take responsibility.

