>>such under acts of legislatures of the provinces in which they reside.
>
>In the U.S., state universities are "public". This doesn't mean that
>they are obliged to provide access to everyone, but it does mean that
>they are bound by the U.S. constitution to, for example, provide
>"modicum of due process".
>
>- Carl

Hi Carl,

The issue of whether or not "The Charter of Rights and Freedoms" (the
Canadian equivalent to the Bill of Rights; often referred to simply
as "The Charter") applies to publically funded universities in Canada
has yet to be tested (definitively) in the Supreme Court of Canada.

A ruling a few years on mandatory retirement suggested that The Charter
did not apply to Canadian public universities, though the substance of
the ruling found that manadatory retirement was a legitimate restriction
on the rights of the individual.

There is another case making its way through the system on a graduate 
student with a history of mental depression being denied a key to
departmental facilities due to past problems.  The university in question
has asserted that The Charter does not apply to them.  Thus we may soon
see a resolution to this issue.

By and large, "due process" on Canadian campuses has been resolved
outside of the legal process, and through the efforts of students,
faculty and staff.

