> convenience.  Strictly speaking, even a CS major could graduate without
> ever logging into a multi-user system.  With the exception of esoteric
> languages or areas of study (like, say, monte-carlo algorithims) that
> require large system support, most CS undergraduate work can be done on
> a PC-- and often is.  


While this generalization may hold at some universities, it is certainly 
by no means true for all.

For example, here at Cornell, the account I'm using now is provided to me
as a CS major.  Many of the classes in the major, some required
for graduation, utilize and require use of the Sun Undergraduate Facility,
including the following:
 
  -- Introduction to Unix and C (1/2 semester)
  -- Data Structures
  -- Typology of Programming Languages
  -- Practicum in Compilers
  -- Practicum in Databases
  -- Practicum in Artificial Intelligence
  -- Applied Logics
  -- Advanced Semantics of Programming Languages
  
None of these classes could be passed without a Sunlab account.  The 
Introduction to Unix and C, and Data Structures, are part of the CS core 
and required of all majors.  Additionally, one practicum (course 
involving substantial programming content) is required; there are two
more than the three listed, but the people who administrate those 
accounts are the same as the ones who adminstrate this one, and it 
seems unlikely that if I was to get my privileges "revoked" here, 
I could get one of the accounts required for those classes.

I'll agree with you that the main portion of this work *could* be done
on a PC, except perhaps the Intro to Unix, but that's not at all the
same as saying that Cornell will *allow* me to do it on my PC.  

Neither am I claiming, BTW, that this is the way it oughta be...

