<JSONDER@auvm.american.edu> writes:

[...]
>I have a few questions about this case.  Is it appropriate for the
>Computer Center to lock these students out of their accounts until
>the students talk to an administrator?  Does changing their passwords
>so they can't login constitute a "suspension"?  What role should
>administrators play in regulating the IRC?

Personally, I don't think locking students out of their account is a
good idea unless they are blatanty abusing it.  What abuse of their
account is depends entirely upon the policy at each university and even
as far as department or faculty.  Ideally, each student would sign a
legal contract which would clearly state what is abuse and accepted
behaviour.  What locking out students does achieve is motivating them
to regain access using less than legal means (in most scenarios).

There is also the problem of students not being told what is acceptable,
students are 'turned loose' on the net with little idea of computer
ethics.  Its this ignorance and lack of respect which causes problems
to arise.  If students have read such a document and/or signed a legal
contract detailing what is and isn't acceptable then they have no
recourse in which to demand their account back after it has been
suspended.

As someone with a good understanding of computer security, a suspended
account is not one where the password has been changed.  The most
curtious behaviour I've seen to this end is a special shell which
displays a message explaining the problem and the solution (meet with
computer administrators) and give them a chace to read it (ie pause at
the end).  This is about the only way that students will respect, they're
not likely to acknowledge email if they think they can get away with
ignoring it or pretend it didn't arrive.

To me, an administrator who is 'regulating' IRC is more or less regulating
who is using the Internet.  People who demonstrate on IRC that they
can't behave maturely most likely aren't going to act mature in any
other sphere either.  The biggest problem I see with this is freshers
coming straight from high school and thinking its a big game and after
that the seniors who like to pick on freshers.  If someone can't behave
then they should be sent off to the cooler for a while and given time
to think whether they'd rather be part of the Internet community or
be an annoyance.  What many of them don't realize is that when they
play on IRC (or the net) it costs money and its not necessarily going
to be them or the University they attend who pays (this is somewhat
similar to having to pay for advertising email being sent to you over
the net - see com-priv discussion for more info).

Well thats a rather long reply to a rather short question but its not
a simple answer since there are many factors to be considered.  I hope
this helps.

Darren.

