I should add an update to the above post, which I made four months ago.  
PL102-561 also moves the fences in wrt to subject matter which it may be a 
felony to infringe.  The prior law limited felony infringement to audiovisual 
works and sound recordings.  Criminal infringements of other types of works 
were, at most, a misdemeanor.  PL102-561 removed any distinctions of types of 
work.  So while it did not expressly add software, it removed the prior 
express limitation to audiovisual works and sound recordings.

So the "fences were moved in" in two respects:

 1) Now _any_ type of work may be feloniously infringed;
 2) The requisit number of copies required to satisfy the requirements for a 
felony is now lowered to 10, and must have a market value of $2500 or greater.

