In <C3CEvK.3nL@techbook.com> jamesd@techbook.com (James Deibele) writes:

>I would agree that a library or copyshop shouldn't be held accountable
>for most illegal copying.  But if there's a copier that's being used to
>do this over and over and the copyshop knows about it, aren't they
>guilty of being an accessory?

Each time that you commit a crime you are in jeopardy.  Each time that
I find out you committed a crime and don't report it, I am in
jeopardy.  But if you commit a crime and I don't find out about it,
I am not in jeopardy.

Personally I have never found out about copyrighted material
inappropriately posted to any newsgroup to which we subscribe.  (I have
not been asked to subscribe to a.b.p or other high risk groups.)

If this does happen, I plan to complain to the source and to the news
masters involved.  I also plan to delete the material from my spool to
avoid anyone at my site displaying the material (and thus violating the
copyright).  If I learn the apparent identity of the poster and of the
copyright holder, then I plan to contact the copyright holder with the
information.

If a.b.p or some other high risk group is of value to our company in
the future, I plan to subscribe to it and take these steps whenever I
find out that a copyright violation occurred.  It would be a pain in
the ass and I don't expect it to happen.  But it would be the right
thing to do.

I don't doubt that I would not be reporting all copyright violations
occurring at my site because I would not be finding out about all of
them.  But I can only be held responsible for the ones I find out
about.
