------------------------------------------------
THE NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF SCHOLARS

     for reasoned scholarship in a free society

     The National Association of Scholars (N.A.S.) is an organization of 
professors, graduate students and college administrators committed to rational 
discourse as the foundation of academic life in a free and democratic society. 
The N.A.S. works to enrich the substance and strengthen the integrity of 
scholarship and teaching, convinced that only through an informed understanding
of the Western intellectual heritage and the realities of the contemporary 
world, can citizen and scholar be equipped to sustain our civilization's 
achievements. In light of these objectives, the National Association of 
Scholars is deeply concerned about the widening currency within the academy of 
perspectives which reflexively denigrate the values and institutions of our 
society. Because such tendencies are often dogmatic in character, and 
indifferent to both logic and evidence, they also tend to undermine the basis 
for coherent scholarly dialogue. Recognizing the significance of this problem, 
the National Association of Scholars encourages a renewed assertiveness among 
academics who value reason and open intellectual life.

N.A.S. programs seek to:

-enhance the quality and content of the curriculum;

-resist the ideological misuse of teaching and scholarship;

-maintain rigorous standards in research, teaching and academic 
self-governance;

-provide informed comment on issues and trends in higher education;

-encourage intellectual balance and realism in campus debate on contemporary 
issues;

-preserve academic freedom and the free exchange of ideas on and off the 
campus;

-create professional forums in which curriculum issues can be discussed in a 
responsible and sophisticated way;

-build an academic leadership dedicated to the ideal of reasoned scholarship 
and

-recall higher education to its classic function of grounding students in the 
rich heritage of their civilization.

______________
N.A.S. Programs

     The N.A.S. encourages the formation of state and local faculty groups 
supportive of its educational philosophy. These affiliates work to inform the 
academic and general publics about regional trends in higher education, 
stimulate intelligent discussion of academic issues arising at institutions 
within their area, and promote curriculum and faculty development. The 
affiliates also organize intramural gatherings and other outreach programs 
designed to increase membership, build scholarly networks, and create a sense 
of academic fellowship among those in agreement with N.A.S. goals.
     The N.A.S.' quarterly journal "Academic Questions" is published in 
conjunction with the Transaction Consortium. "Academic Questions" is designed 
to explore problems and trends relating to the quality of scholarship and 
teaching, the politicization of academic life, and other curricular and 
educational matters.
     The N.A.S. encourages the formation of professional and disciplinary 
organizations sharing its educational purposes; assists student groups and 
publications addressing educational issues in a responsible manner; organizes 
panels, conferences and professional symposia on topics pertinent to its 
concerns, arranging for the dissemination of their proceedings; acts to form 
links with like-minded academics outside the United States; and promotes 
studies documenting and analyzing the state of academic life.
     The N.A.S. operates a speakers bureau on educational issues. Speakers are 
available for appearances in faculty, student, community and media forums.
     N.A.S. fellowships recognize and support scholars making unusual efforts 
to enhance the academic environment in which they work. Fellowships serve to 
sponsor activities designed to increase participation in, and awareness of, 
N.A.S. programs; further inquiries into the sociology of scholarship and the 
dynamics of higher education, particularly as these relate to problematic 
aspects of academic life; promote research into areas of scholarly controversy 
likely to contribute to the strengthening of course content; encourage the 
development of curriculum materials informed by responsible academic judgment; 
and assist in the organization of disciplinary, campus, or community forums for
the discussion of educational and public policy, aimed at raising the 
intellectual and ethical level at which these are treated.
