In article <9303071440.AA02191@bottom.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>, John E Hinton <jhinton@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu> writes:
[on the National Association of Scholars (a group I've never heard of
before, but I guess I'm just not a scholar :-)...the same old tried and
true "traditionalists" in the anti-PC guise, as if PC-ness were in and
of itself a bad thing (tm), the actual ideas expressed aren't individually
debated]

>     for reasoned scholarship in a free society

Sounds good so far...of course this is the first line...and of course
later it don't see much evidence for "scholarship", just a bunch of
retoric and dogmatic statements, from the good conservative side
rather than the evil liberal side (of course).

>     The National Association of Scholars (N.A.S.) is an organization of 
>professors, graduate students and college administrators committed to rational 
>discourse as the foundation of academic life in a free and democratic society. 
>The N.A.S. works to enrich the substance and strengthen the integrity of 
>scholarship and teaching, convinced that only through an informed understanding
>of the Western intellectual heritage and the realities of the contemporary 
>world, can citizen and scholar be equipped to sustain our civilization's 
>achievements.

Still sounds reasonable; a little western culture-centric, but hey, that IS
the basis for the _majority_ of the US culture.  Note I said majority,
unfortunately this group seems to imply exclusivity.

>In light of these objectives, the National Association of 
>Scholars is deeply concerned about the widening currency within the academy of 
>perspectives which reflexively denigrate the values and institutions of our 
>society.

Danger Will Robinson, Danger.  Code words approaching... What the hell is
"reflexively denigrate" and just who gets to define the dividing line
between that and "discuss from an alternative viewpoint".  This is PC-ness
from a _conservative_ political viewpoint.  It's equally as bad as the
stuff more traditionally labelled "PC", but PC never the less.

>Because such tendencies are often dogmatic in character, and 
>indifferent to both logic and evidence, they also tend to undermine the basis 
>for coherent scholarly dialogue.

Now, please everyone note:  this is a very dogmatic statement being made
above, with no sign of evidence not justification for the logic without
at least some more explicit examples of what they mean.  Sorry, I too
happen to disagree with the far-left's attempt to squelch dialogue about
certain issues, but this article so far has touched all my hot buttons
in that it just comes across as the EXACT same thing with a bunch of
"conservative" (ie. far-right wing) code words replacing the evil thought
of those other anti-intellectuals (the nasty liberals).  All this with
the more moderate middle being squashed in the crunch.  

>Recognizing the significance of this problem, 
>the National Association of Scholars encourages a renewed assertiveness among 
>academics who value reason and open intellectual life.

>N.A.S. programs seek to:

Lots of mother and apple pie statements follow...

>-enhance the quality and content of the curriculum;

Ah, I think this IS the exact same code words that those nasty PC'ers
use when promoting *their* agenda...to make a judgement call I'd
have to see something much more explicit in what is meant.

>-resist the ideological misuse of teaching and scholarship;

And again, who decides what is "ideological misuse" and what isn't?
Or are these people just going to end up replacing one set of 
ideologies with their own by ruling that certain lines of thought
are off-limits?

>-maintain rigorous standards in research, teaching and academic 
>self-governance;

Strawman.  I don't see anyone arguing for lowering them.  Again this
strikes me as a bunch of code words for "I don't agree with that person's
line of {research,teaching,...} so they MUST be lowering the standards".
Until someone can put forth definitive and OBJECTIVE measures of said
standards I think this will just denigrate into a bunch of finger pointing.

>-provide informed comment on issues and trends in higher education;

Who decides what is "informed" comment and decides that it isn't currently
being provided?

>-encourage intellectual balance and realism in campus debate on contemporary 
>issues;

Again, this hits me as the same ol' statement that "the other guy" isn't
being balanced or realistic.  Hardly an objective statement.

>-preserve academic freedom and the free exchange of ideas on and off the 
>campus;

Strawman, except at the extremes of _both_ sides of the spectrum; I whole
heartedly agree with these ideals (along with mother and apple pie).  But
I fear that this will disinegrate into preserving the free exchange by
trying to crush the opposition.

>-create professional forums in which curriculum issues can be discussed in a 
>responsible and sophisticated way;
>-build an academic leadership dedicated to the ideal of reasoned scholarship 
>and

more apple pie.

>-recall higher education to its classic function of grounding students in the 
>rich heritage of their civilization.

Ah, another "conservative" bunch of code words.  Every time I see this
argument phrased this way I get the heebie-jeebies.  Those
"anti-intellectuals" (sarcasm!) that promote the expansion of woman/ethnic
studies would strongly argue that they ARE promoting the grounding in the
rich heritage of their civilization; but it always seems that the people
who make statements like the above have the implicit assignment that
"heritage of their civilization" is identically equal to "dead white guys".
If the NAS was to come right out and state whether indeed that exactly
what they meant then I could at least respect them for their honesty.
Alas, from past experience I have to take this statement as just the
conservative equivalent of PC:  classics == good, new-stuff == bad.


Overall, I have to agree with their STATED objectives of trying to
depoliticize scholarship in general.  Unfortunately, to me at least,
they come off as evasive in _exactly_ what they mean.  There are enough
things I see as conservative code words to make me very suspicious.


-robert (hatcher@msupa.pa.msu.edu)


